     "description": "Three Thames sites named in Snow 1855. Coordinates are of the landmarks he names, geocoded from OpenStreetMap via Nominatim; the works themselves are not surveyed here."
    },
    "provenance": {
     "what": "The three Thames sites the two companies drew from, as Snow names them, and the tidal limit that separates them",
     "citation": {
      "type": "book",
      "authors": [
       {
        "family": "Snow",
        "given": "John"
       }
      ],
      "title": "On the Mode of Communication of Cholera",
      "place": "London",
      "publisher": "John Churchill"
     },
     "published": "1855",
     "edition": {
      "statement": "2nd",
      "published": "2024-02-07",
      "publisher": "Project Gutenberg, eBook #72894"
     },
     "retrieved": "2026-10-27",
     "represents": "1849–1854",
     "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894.txt",
     "licence": "none-applies",
     "licenceSource": "An 1855 publication: in the public domain, which is a positive claim that no terms attach rather than an absence of information.",
     "note": "THE POSITIONS ARE LANDMARKS, NOT WORKS. Snow locates the intakes by relation — \"about half a mile above Vauxhall Bridge\", \"opposite Hungerford Market\" — and each point marks the named landmark with the relation recorded beside it. Landmark coordinates were obtained from OpenStreetMap via the Nominatim geocoder, © OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL. Three coordinates are not a substantial extract of that database, so no share-alike obligation is asserted; the attribution is recorded because the figures came from somewhere and a reader should know where. The tidal limit is Teddington Lock, which Snow names himself and twice — once as the point governing what reaches the sea, once in placing the Lambeth Company’s supply above it. An earlier version of this note said he does not name it; that was wrong, and it was written after a search of the source missed the passages because the text is hard wrapped and the phrase spanned two lines."
    },
